{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "line-tokens-and-storage",
      "text": "With the default ScanLines splitter and a four-byte buffer, let the reader return \"a\\r\\nb\", then \"c\\n\\r\", then io.EOF. The first Scan returns \"a\". Save both Bytes() and Text(). Before reading the second chunk, the next Scan compacts the unread \"b\" onto the saved slice's backing array. It returns \"bc\", the saved slice now reads \"b\", and the saved string still reads \"a\". The third Scan reaches EOF and returns a non-nil empty token for the lone trailing carriage return. The fourth returns false with Err() == nil.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 368,
        "fragment": "return len(data), dropCR(data), nil"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should ScanLines strip a lone trailing carriage return when its documented end-of-line marker requires a newline?"
    },
    {
      "id": "read-error-order",
      "text": "Start with an empty Scanner and a splitter that consumes and returns \"x\" while atEOF is false and returns a custom error S whenever atEOF is true. If the reader returns \"x\" together with a non-EOF error R, the first split sees atEOF == true and the first Scan returns false with Err() == R. If the reader returns \"x\" with nil and reports R on the next read, the first Scan returns \"x\" and the second returns false with Err() == R. Replacing R with io.EOF preserves the difference in token delivery, but Err() reports S because the splitter can replace a recorded io.EOF.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 150,
        "fragment": "s.split(s.buf[s.start:s.end], s.err != nil)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the SplitFunc contract explicitly define atEOF to include non-EOF read errors?"
    },
    {
      "id": "final-token",
      "text": "When a split invocation returns ErrFinalToken itself with a negative advance and a non-nil empty slice, Scan stores that slice and returns true without validating or applying the advance. Every later Scan returns false without invoking the splitter or reader, and Bytes() still returns the final slice. Returning a nil slice with the sentinel makes the current Scan return false and clears Bytes(). Err() is nil if the stored error is nil or io.EOF, and still reports any non-EOF reader error recorded before the split invocation.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 152,
        "fragment": "if err == ErrFinalToken {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Does the sentinel's promise to stop with no error describe only the sentinel itself, or should it also suppress a previously recorded reader error?"
    },
    {
      "id": "full-buffer-order",
      "text": "Configure a fresh Scanner with Buffer(make([]byte, 4), 4) and keep ScanLines. If the reader returns \"abcd\" with nil and would report EOF on the next read, Scan needs more input and returns false with ErrTooLong before making that read. A second Scan passes the retained bytes to ScanLines with atEOF == true, returns \"abcd\", and leaves Err() equal to ErrTooLong. If the reader instead returns \"abcd\" together with io.EOF, the first Scan returns the four-byte token and the next returns false with Err() == nil.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 200,
        "fragment": "if len(s.buf) >= s.maxTokenSize || len(s.buf) > maxInt/2 {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should ErrTooLong prevent token delivery on later calls given the unrecoverable-stop wording, and does the documented strict size bound apply only when the splitter still needs more input at capacity?"
    },
    {
      "id": "split-after-exhaustion",
      "text": "Let the first read return \"ab\" with io.EOF and let the splitter return (1, nil, nil) on that input. Scan advances past \"a\", clears both buffer indices, and returns false with Err() == nil. The next Scan invokes the splitter with empty data and atEOF == true without reading again or presenting the discarded \"b\". If the splitter now returns (0, []byte(\"fallback\"), nil), that Scan returns true with \"fallback\".",
      "citation": {
        "line": 185,
        "fragment": "s.end = 0"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: After false at EOF, is the splitter supposed to be callable again, and may it produce a token that the caller would miss in a standard Scan loop?"
    },
    {
      "id": "no-progress-guards",
      "text": "With buffered input and no stored error, a splitter that repeatedly returns (0, []byte(\"x\"), nil) makes every Scan return true on the same input. No further read occurs and the token counter resets on each call. With a stored EOF and the counter initially zero, the same split result increments the counter on every call and panics on the first call beyond maxConsecutiveEmptyReads, even though the token is nonempty. Separately, a reader that repeatedly returns (0, nil) is stopped on the first read beyond that limit. The splitter then receives atEOF == true, and if it yields no token, Scan returns false with Err() == io.ErrNoProgress.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 168,
        "fragment": "if s.err == nil || advance > 0 {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the panic documentation describe non-advancing non-nil tokens after a stored error, or should the guard also cover splitters that stall before EOF?"
    }
  ]
}
